<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  JawaTerrain_HorrorWastes.xml           Jawa_Patches · authored 2026-08-23
  ============================================================================

  HORROR_WASTES_COLD_TERRAIN_1.

  HorrorWastes (Horrors (Continued)) was authored for a warm dry band. On
  Ash'karr it holds 468 nightside tiles at -74.9 .. -33.9 C, median -49.3,
  scattered through Deadstone, Umbra and Ammonia Flats. Its ground was
  Sand 0.01-0.45 / Soil 0.45-0.90 / SoilRich 0.90-0.99 - warm sand and
  farmland loam on the deep nightside.

  THE READ (DECIDE's brief): dark biological muck breaking through frost.
  NOT a snowfield. Brighter and dirtier than the near-black crags around it
  (AB_RockyCrags is [30,29,34]); broken rather than the flat pale sheet of
  sea ice (Ice is [155,164,172]).

  ==========================================================================
  TWO CORRECTIONS TO THE BRIEF, both measured, both load-bearing
  ==========================================================================

  1. AB_Ice IS NOT A TERRAIN DEF. It is a TEXTURE, Terrain/Surfaces/AB_Ice,
     worn by AB_SolidPropane and AB_PropaneLake. The brief offered it or
     AB_PackedIce; only AB_PackedIce exists, and its texture is
     Terrain/Surfaces/AB_WhiteIce - measured [189,193,201], paler than the
     [151,167,191] the brief expected. Still the bare-frozen tier: distinct
     from the crags, and the sea ice it resembles is a neighbouring BIOME,
     never an adjacent cell on the same map.

  2. AB_DarkMud HAS FERTILITY 0, and so does AB_PackedIce. The brief put
     AB_DarkMud at the TOP of the fertility order. Vanilla PlantBase sets
     <fertilityMin>0.7</fertilityMin> (Plants_Bases.xml:23) and
     PlantUtility.cs:85 refuses to spawn a plant where
     fertilityGrid.FertilityAt(c) < fertilityMin. Six of the eight plants
     BiomeFlora_Ashkarr.xml just assigned to this biome inherit that 0.7 -
     only HorrorWeb and AA_RottingMound declare 0. Ending the ramp on a
     sterile terrain would have left the new roster unable to root anywhere
     and reported nothing.

     So the ramp ends on AB_FertileMud instead: fertility 1.0, measured
     [55,42,33] - dark brown organic muck, the same read, and the flora
     actually grows in it. AB_DarkMud [33,26,20] keeps the dominant middle
     band, where its sterility is the point: near-black frozen organics
     with nothing living in them.

  AB_Obsidian [46,46,46] stays rejected - too close to the crags' [30,29,34]
  to read as a different place.

  Colours are means over the real textures, design/Jawa/fauna/biome_palette.json.

  NOT THIS ITEM'S JOB: animalDensity stays 3.6 and the cast belongs to
  BIOME_CREATURE_CAST_1. plantDensity stays 0.5 - it was flagged as high for
  a roster of ONE (Plant_Agave), and BiomeFlora_Ashkarr.xml has since given
  the biome eight plants, so the complaint no longer holds.

  Both operations are wrapped in a Conditional: a Replace that matches
  nothing is a RED ERROR, not a no-op, and MayRequire only checks the mod,
  never that the def survived.
  ==========================================================================
-->
<Patch>

  <!-- ==== the ground ================================================= -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="HorrorWastes"]/terrainsByFertility</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
      <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="HorrorWastes"]/terrainsByFertility</xpath>
      <value>
        <terrainsByFertility>
          <!-- bare frozen ground: pale, sterile, the uncommon extreme -->
          <li>
            <terrain>AB_PackedIce</terrain>
            <min>-999</min>
            <max>0.30</max>
          </li>
          <!-- frost breaking over stone - fertility 0.7, so the roster roots here -->
          <li>
            <terrain>AB_SnowOverRocks</terrain>
            <min>0.30</min>
            <max>0.50</max>
          </li>
          <!-- near-black frozen organics - dominant, and nothing grows in it -->
          <li>
            <terrain>AB_DarkMud</terrain>
            <min>0.50</min>
            <max>0.80</max>
          </li>
          <!-- the deep muck - fertility 1.0, where the horror flora actually lives -->
          <li>
            <terrain>AB_FertileMud</terrain>
            <min>0.80</min>
            <max>999</max>
          </li>
        </terrainsByFertility>
      </value>
    </match>
  </Operation>

  <!-- ==== the description, which still said "a dry region" =========== -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="HorrorWastes"]/description</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
      <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="HorrorWastes"]/description</xpath>
      <value>
        <description>A frozen region, contorted by alien fauna and flora to be unrecognizable. Dark organic matter breaks through the frost in patches, refusing to die in a cold that should have killed it. A terrible place of disease, Horrors and suffering. It is difficult indeed to survive in such a place.</description>
      </value>
    </match>
  </Operation>

  <!--
    🔴 THE SMEAR. HorrorWastes' own world texture is the reason this biome appears to
    bleed across the planet. Measured against the shipped art:

      HorrorBiome.png   1024x1024  luminance std 20.06  8x8 block std 19.75  p1..p99 90.6
      Core Ocean         500x500   luminance std  3.40  8x8 block std  2.83  p1..p99 13.0
      Core Desert        500x500   luminance std  0.00  8x8 block std  0.00  - ONE FLAT COLOUR

    ⭐ WHY FLATNESS IS THE CONTRACT. The world terrain mesh carries NO texture UVs:
    WorldDrawLayer_Terrain.Regenerate writes ELEVATION into `uvs`, and BiomeDef assigns
    the PNG straight onto the shared WorldTerrain material. The shader therefore projects
    the texture across the SPHERE rather than per tile. A near-flat texture hides that
    projection completely; large-scale blotches ride it and stretch. That stretching IS
    the artifact. 7.92% of the original's pixels are below luminance 16, and it is pure
    greyscale (channel spread 1.0) - the only biome texture on the planet at 1024x1024.

    ⚠️ The mod ships NO Textures folder: the PNG lives inside the AssetBundle
    `Mlie_Horrors`, which is why a file search never finds it. Extracted copy:
    observed/inventory/bundle_textures/mlie.horrors/textures/world/biomes/horrorbiome.png

    Our replacement keeps the ORIGINAL's fine grain - FFT high-pass, zeroing radial
    frequencies below 12, which preserves tiling exactly - and remaps it onto a COLD
    dark base. Cold, not the warm grey a first pass produced, because this file's own
    terrain ramp above commits the biome to AB_PackedIce and AB_DarkMud: pale ice over
    near-black frozen muck. Measured result 512x512, Lstd 2.99, block std 2.63,
    p1..p99 13.1, zero pixels below 16, wrap seams 0.48/0.46 against an interior 0.41.
  -->

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationConditional">
    <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="HorrorWastes"]/texture</xpath>
    <match Class="PatchOperationReplace">
      <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="HorrorWastes"]/texture</xpath>
      <value><texture>World/Biomes/Jawa_HorrorWastes</texture></value>
    </match>
    <nomatch Class="PatchOperationAdd">
      <xpath>/Defs/BiomeDef[defName="HorrorWastes"]</xpath>
      <value><texture>World/Biomes/Jawa_HorrorWastes</texture></value>
    </nomatch>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
